.TH FEMESH 2
.SH NAME
femesh: parse, nnodes, nelem, nodeid, nodeijk, nodecoord, isboundary, elemnodes \- a structured hex8 finite-element mesh
.SH SYNOPSIS
.EX
include "femesh.m";
femesh := load Femesh Femesh->PATH;

(g, err) := femesh->parse("mesh 20x20x20 domain 1x1x1");
n := femesh->nnodes(g);
e := femesh->nelem(g);
nodes := femesh->elemnodes(g, 0);
.EE
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Femesh
describes a structured brick of hex8 (trilinear 8-node hexahedral)
elements \(em the mesh shape a real finite-element assembly needs:
nodes, elements, and which nodes each element touches, not just a
uniform array of values the way
.IR mesh (2)'s
own
.B Grid
is for
.IR pde (2)'s
finite-difference stencils.
.B Femesh
is the domain half of
.IR fem (2),
split out the same way
.IR mesh (2)
is split out from
.IR pde (2).
.PP
The grammar is one line:
.EX
mesh NXxNYxNZ [domain WxHxD]
.EE
giving
.I nx
by
.I ny
by
.I nz
elements; domain defaults to
.B 1x1x1
if omitted.
.B Parse
returns
.RI ( nil ", " error )
on a malformed line.
.PP
A
.B Grid
has
.RI ( nx +1)*( ny +1)*( nz +1)
nodes and
.IR nx * ny * nz
elements.
.B Nodeid
maps a node's
.RI ( i , j , k )
grid position to its flat id;
.B Nodeijk
and
.B Nodecoord
invert that, giving grid position or physical
.RI ( x , y , z )
coordinates from an id.
.B Isboundary
reports whether a node lies on any of the brick's six outer faces \(em
the set a Dirichlet boundary condition applies to.
.B Elemnodes
gives the 8 global node ids of element
.I e
in standard hex8 local order: the bottom face counterclockwise, then
the top face the same way, each node directly above the one 4 below
it \(em the order
.IR fem (2)'s
element stiffness matrix is written against.
.SH SOURCE
.B /module/femesh.m
.br
.B /appl/lib/femesh.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR mesh (2),
.IR sparse (2),
.IR fem (2)
